# ADR-001: Adopt Two-Tier Load Economics for AI Documentation
**Status:** Accepted
**Date:** 2026-04-30
**Signed:** WH

## Context

Our AI documentation system was monolithic: every session loaded STARTUP_AI.{{AI}}ai, the full readme_AI.{{AI}}ai (including all historical THREADS and HANDOFF_LOG entries), and the HI Mode charter in full — regardless of what task was being worked on. A one-line config edit loaded the same context budget as a deep architectural session.

Research backing the problem:
- Context length alone degrades LLM accuracy 13–85% as input grows, even when added content is relevant.
- Frontier models reliably follow ~150–200 distinct instructions. Claude Code's built-in prompt uses ~50, leaving a real but finite budget.
- LLM attention is U-shaped (lost-in-the-middle): instructions in the middle of long documents are under-attended even when most relevant.
- Path-scoped rules reduce rule-token usage sharply because most rules are inactive for most tasks.

## Decision

Adopt a two-tier load model across all templates and working repos:

**Tier 1 (always loaded, budget-constrained):**
- CLAUDE.md ≤50 lines — path-to-rule table + Tier 1 pointers
- AGENTS.md ≤90 lines — cross-tool wiring mirror
- STARTUP_AI.{{AI}}ai — boot sequence, lean
- readme_AI.{{AI}}ai — active state only (open THREADS + latest 1–2 HANDOFFs)
- ai_context/ai_rules.{{AI}}ai — project-wide hard constraints ≤100 lines
- ai_context/glossary.{{AI}}ai — terminology, anti-synonym
- .claude/rules/<domain>.{{AI}}ai — path-scoped, loaded only when matching files are touched

**Tier 2 (on-demand reference, no size cap because loading is selective):**
- ai_context/decisions/ — ADRs
- ai_context/readme_AI_archive.{{AI}}ai — closed THREADS + historical HANDOFFs
- ai_context/architecture/, auth/, api/, etc. — domain deep-dives
- ai_context/ski_lift_log.{{AI}}ai — tooling recommendations
- ai_context/anti_patterns.{{AI}}ai, boundaries.{{AI}}ai, debt.{{AI}}ai

## Alternatives Considered

| Option | Why Rejected |
|--------|-------------|
| Keep monolithic load, prune files periodically | Pruning loses history. Doesn't solve the mid-document attention problem. Manual maintenance burden. |
| Separate repos for AI context | Too much friction. Session startup cost too high. |
| AI reads everything on demand (no always-loaded set) | Boot becomes unbounded. Loses the "just enough context" guarantee at session start. |

## Consequences

- Session boot is faster and within instruction budget.
- Historical THREADS and old HANDOFFs remain accessible (Tier 2) but don't tax every turn.
- Path-scoped rules require CLAUDE.md to maintain a path-to-rule table — small ongoing maintenance cost.
- Requires restructuring existing repos' CLAUDE.md, readme_AI.{{AI}}ai, and ai_context/ layout.

